        <h1>Recognizing Regions in Gait Data with K-Means and PCA</h1>

<h2>Overview</h2>

<p>This document describes the implementation of two unsupervised learning methods for recognizing different regions in accelerometer gait data (ax, ay, az):</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>K-Means Clustering</strong> - Groups similar motion patterns into 4 clusters</li>
  <li><strong>PCA Anomaly Detection</strong> - Identifies unusual motion patterns</li>
</ol>

<p>Both methods use sliding window feature extraction to analyze the time series data.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Data Format</h2>

<p>The code expects a CSV file or DataFrame with three columns:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>ax</code> - Acceleration in x-axis</li>
  <li><code>ay</code> - Acceleration in y-axis</li>
  <li><code>az</code> - Acceleration in z-axis</li>
</ul>

<p>The <code>load_accelerometer_data()</code> function automatically detects common column naming variations.</p>

<hr>

<h2>Feature Extraction</h2>

<h3>Sliding Window Approach</h3>

<p>Features are extracted using a sliding window with:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Window size</strong>: 50 samples (default)</li>
  <li><strong>Overlap</strong>: 50% (step size = 25 samples)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Features per Window</h3>

<p>For each axis (ax, ay, az), 7 features are computed:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Mean</li>
  <li>Standard deviation</li>
  <li>Minimum</li>
  <li>Maximum</li>
  <li>25th percentile</li>
  <li>75th percentile</li>
  <li>RMS (Root Mean Square)</li>
</ul>

<p>Plus 3 magnitude features:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Mean magnitude: <code>sqrt(ax² + ay² + az²)</code></li>
  <li>Std of magnitude</li>
  <li>Range of magnitude (max - min)</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Total: 24 features per window</strong></p>

<hr>

<h2>Method 1: K-Means Clustering</h2>

<h3>How It Works</h3>

<pre><code>detector = AccelerometerRegionDetector(df)
labels_kmeans, features_kmeans = detector.kmeans_segmentation(n_clusters=4, window_size=50)</code></pre>

<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Extract features from sliding windows</li>
  <li>Standardize features (zero mean, unit variance)</li>
  <li>Apply K-means with 4 clusters</li>
  <li>Expand window labels to original time series length</li>
</ol>

<h3>Parameters</h3>

<ul>
  <li><code>n_clusters</code> (default: 5) - Number of regions to detect. <strong>Set to 4 for gait data.</strong></li>
  <li><code>window_size</code> (default: 50) - Size of sliding window</li>
  <li><code>random_state</code> (fixed: 42) - For reproducible results</li>
  <li><code>n_init</code> (fixed: 10) - Number of K-means initializations</li>
</ul>

<h3>Output</h3>

<ul>
  <li><code>labels_kmeans</code>: Array of cluster labels for each time point</li>
  <li><code>features_kmeans</code>: Scaled feature matrix</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example Output</h3>

<pre><code>============================================================
APPROACH 1: K-Means Clustering on Features
============================================================
Detected 4 different regions
Cluster distribution: [13  5 13  7]</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2>Method 2: PCA Anomaly Detection</h2>

<h3>How It Works</h3>

<pre><code>detector = AccelerometerRegionDetector(df)
labels_pca, recon_error = detector.pca_anomaly_detection(
    window_size=50, 
    n_components=3, 
    threshold=2.0
)</code></pre>

<p><strong>Steps:</strong></p>
<ol>
  <li>Extract features from sliding windows</li>
  <li>Standardize features</li>
  <li>Apply PCA to reduce dimensionality to 3 components</li>
  <li>Reconstruct features from PCA components</li>
  <li>Calculate reconstruction error</li>
  <li>Label windows as normal (0) or anomalous (1) based on threshold</li>
</ol>

<h3>Parameters</h3>

<ul>
  <li><code>window_size</code> (default: 50) - Size of sliding window</li>
  <li><code>n_components</code> (default: 3) - Number of principal components to keep</li>
  <li><code>threshold</code> (default: 2.0) - Z-score threshold for anomaly detection</li>
</ul>

<h3>Anomaly Detection Logic</h3>

<pre><code>mean_error = np.mean(reconstruction_error)
std_error = np.std(reconstruction_error)
labels = reconstruction_error > (mean_error + threshold * std_error)</code></pre>

<p>Windows with reconstruction error > <code>mean + 2.0 * std</code> are marked as anomalous.</p>

<h3>Output</h3>

<ul>
  <li><code>labels_pca</code>: Binary labels (0=normal, 1=anomalous)</li>
  <li><code>recon_error</code>: Reconstruction error for each window</li>
</ul>

<h3>Example Output</h3>

<pre><code>============================================================
APPROACH 4: PCA-based Anomaly Detection
============================================================
Variance explained: 92.47%
Normal regions: 36
Anomalous regions: 2</code></pre>

<hr>

<h2>Visualization</h2>

<p>The <code>visualize_results()</code> function creates a 4-panel plot:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>ax axis</strong> - With colored regions overlay</li>
  <li><strong>ay axis</strong> - With colored regions overlay</li>
  <li><strong>az axis</strong> - With colored regions overlay</li>
  <li><strong>Magnitude</strong> - <code>sqrt(ax² + ay² + az²)</code> with regions</li>
</ol>

<pre><code>fig = visualize_results(df, labels_kmeans, "K-Means Segmentation")
plt.savefig('output.png', dpi=150, bbox_inches='tight')</code></pre>

